#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# carmichael_lambda

#% text
# `carmichael_lambda(n)` gives the Carmichael function, the exponent of the multiplicative group modulo `n`: the least `m` with `mod(a^m, n) == 1` for every `a` prime to `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It divides `euler_phi(n)` and equals it exactly when `n` has a primitive root.

#% item
# `carmichael_lambda` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
carmichael_lambda(8)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
carmichael_lambda(15)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
carmichael_lambda(561)
#% output
#= 80

#% cell
carmichael_lambda(range(10))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 2, 6, 2, 6, 4]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Every unit raised to `carmichael_lambda(n)` is 1:

#% cell
union(table(power_mod(a, carmichael_lambda(24), 24), a, select(range(24), a => gcd(a, 24) == 1)))
#% output
#= [1]

#% text
# Compared with `euler_phi`:

#% cell
[carmichael_lambda(24), euler_phi(24)]
#% output
#= [2, 8]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `euler_phi`, `multiplicative_order`, `primitive_root`
